    "description": "Custom software development builds applications around a defined set of users and business processes. Iranian service descriptions illustrate work ranging from internal dashboards to integrated web applications, while international security guidance supplies a framework for examining how such software is developed and verified.",
    "tags": ["Custom software", "Software engineering", "Business workflows", "Application security", "Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T18:16:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T18:16:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Custom Software Development in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand custom software development in Iran through documented services, workflow design, localization, and security verification."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Software organized around a specific problem",
        "content": "Custom development begins with a defined problem and its users. Radroza's Persian-language service page offers an illustrative Iranian example: it describes dashboards, internal tools, and web applications, with work progressing from scope and architecture through iterative development to deployment. It also describes integration with existing systems where suitable connection methods exist. These are supplier descriptions of available services, not independently verified project outcomes. [s1]\n\nA useful distinction is between a business's requested features and the work those features must accomplish. Consider an illustrative service-request application: its value would depend on whether staff can receive a request, assign responsibility, and follow its resolution. A feature list alone would not establish that the workflow succeeds. This example applies the source's emphasis on users and processes; it does not describe a documented Iranian deployment. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "localization",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Local language is part of the specification",
        "content": "For Persian-language applications, localization includes precise handling of characters and numerical presentation. Unicode's Persian guidance specifies which forms of letters, digits, and separators to use, including distinctions between characters that can appear similar. Its date-formatting discussion also shows that language rules can affect the presentation of month names. [s2]\n\nThe engineering implication is that localization deserves explicit acceptance examples. A project can test representative names, search terms, imported records, and displayed amounts rather than judging language support solely from a screenshot. These are proposed applications of the Unicode guidance, not evidence that Iranian development teams universally follow a particular testing practice. The same distinction matters when reading a supplier's claim to offer localized software: the claim identifies a capability to examine, while a demonstration establishes behavior in a specified case. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "security",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Development and verification answer different questions",
        "content": "Security requires attention throughout development. The US National Institute of Standards and Technology's Secure Software Development Framework describes high-level practices that can be integrated into different development lifecycles. Its stated purpose includes reducing vulnerabilities and addressing their causes. It is a general engineering reference, not an Iranian regulatory requirement. [s3]\n\nVerification needs a more explicit account of what was checked. The OWASP Application Security Verification Standard provides requirements for examining application security controls and can inform procurement specifications. Its guidance recommends including a version when referencing requirement identifiers, because identifiers can change. A meaningful verification report can therefore identify the agreed requirements, the tested application version, and the evidence behind its findings. Such a report supports a bounded conclusion; it is not a guarantee that software contains no vulnerabilities. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "handover",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The application continues after launch",
        "content": "The inspected Iranian service description includes deployment, error monitoring, documentation, and planning for future development. This makes continuity a useful part of understanding custom work: the application needs an operational life beyond its initial delivery. The source describes the supplier's intended service scope, without establishing the quality or duration of support in completed projects. [s1]\n\nTaken together, the evidence suggests an evaluation framework built around workflow fit, language behavior, maintainability, and documented verification. It does not support a national ranking of developers, a standard project price, or a claim about export competitiveness. Custom development is best understood here as a method of addressing a particular organizational need, whose outcome must be assessed through project-specific evidence. [s1][s2][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
