    "description": "Software architecture consulting examines how technical decisions support a system's business goals and quality requirements. Iranian service documentation provides an example of architecture review as a commercial offering, while international references explain architecture descriptions and structured evaluation of competing goals.",
    "tags": ["Software architecture", "Technical consulting", "Architecture evaluation", "System design", "Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T18:16:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T18:16:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Software Architecture Consulting in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore architecture consulting through an Iranian service example, architecture documentation, and structured analysis of technical tradeoffs."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "purpose",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Connecting technical decisions to organizational goals",
        "content": "Architecture consulting concerns the reasoning behind a system's organization and technical choices. Its central questions include whether a design can support the qualities its users and owners need, and which risks follow from the proposed decisions. The Software Engineering Institute's Architecture Tradeoff Analysis Method, or ATAM, evaluates architecture against quality goals and examines how those goals interact. It supplies a useful international reference for understanding the work, without establishing how widely the method is used in Iran. [s1]\n\nFor an international reader, this makes the subject more concrete than a catalogue of technologies. A design can be assessed against an operational concern, such as how a service responds when a dependency fails. The concern, scenario, and proposed response give an evaluator something to examine. The example is illustrative; it is not a finding about an Iranian system. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iranian-example",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A documented service offering",
        "content": "Radroza's architecture-consulting page describes reviews of application structure, dependencies, maintainability, performance bottlenecks, data handling, and scalability. It also offers comparison of technical options and migration planning. The proposed outputs include findings, prioritized recommendations, and a phased roadmap, with implementation support available separately. [s2]\n\nThis is evidence of a supplier presenting architecture review as a service in the Iranian web ecosystem. It does not demonstrate the provider's independence in a particular engagement, validate its expertise, or establish successful outcomes. The distinction is especially useful when reading service pages: descriptions can explain what work is offered, while evaluation requires evidence of what was actually examined and delivered. The inspected page supplies no basis for ranking the national consulting market. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "description",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An architecture description is an artifact",
        "content": "ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 distinguishes an entity's architecture from the description that expresses it. The standard's public abstract addresses the structure and expression of architecture descriptions, including viewpoints and related concepts. It does not prescribe the processes, methods, techniques, or tools used to create them. Only the public abstract was inspected for this article; the full standard was not reviewed. [s3]\n\nThis distinction helps explain why producing a diagram and evaluating a design are separate activities. Documentation makes assumptions and relationships available for discussion. Evaluation asks whether the described decisions address the goals and constraints at issue. A consulting deliverable can usefully include both, but the existence of a polished description alone does not establish that the underlying system will perform as intended. This is an editorial inference from the different scopes of architecture description and evaluation. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "tradeoffs",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Making competing goals visible",
        "content": "The Software Engineering Institute describes ATAM as a process that elicits business drivers, refines quality goals into scenarios, analyzes architectural decisions, and identifies risks and tradeoffs. Its contribution is to expose interactions between desirable properties rather than assume that a design improves every property simultaneously. [s1]\n\nThe simplified diagram below summarizes that evaluation logic. It is not a prescribed Iranian procurement process or a claim that the inspected supplier follows ATAM. For this topic, the practical conclusion is that architecture consulting becomes assessable when it leaves a clear connection between the original concern, the evidence examined, the alternatives considered, and the recommendation. National market size and typical commercial outcomes remain outside the evidence established here. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evaluation-diagram",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "A simplified architecture evaluation",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Business goals\"] -->|\"Guide priorities\"| B[\"Quality scenarios\"]\n  C[\"Architecture description\"] -->|\"Provides decisions to examine\"| D[\"Scenario analysis\"]\n  B -->|\"Provides evaluation cases\"| D\n  D -->|\"Identifies\"| E[\"Risks and tradeoffs\"]\n  E -->|\"Informs\"| F[\"Findings and recommendations\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows show how goals and documented decisions contribute to evaluation. This is a simplified synthesis of ATAM's evaluation logic, not its complete procedure or evidence of Iranian adoption. [s1]",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      }
    ],
